WEBVTT

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:05.026 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
A shopper buys tomato soup. Imagine
that the receipt says chicken noodle.

00:00:05.026 --> 00:00:07.309 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Could the model tell which
flavor the camera saw?

00:00:07.709 --> 00:00:10.010 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Amazon's Just Walk Out uses cameras

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:13.021 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
and shelf sensors to follow
the items shoppers take.

00:00:13.421 --> 00:00:18.519 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Researchers can study a shopping trip
inside a simulated store, like Sari Sandbox.

00:00:18.919 --> 00:00:23.861 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Virtual try-on shows shoppers how a pair
of glasses would look on their own face.

00:00:24.261 --> 00:00:27.284 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We studied SigLIP2, a vision-language model

00:00:27.284 --> 00:00:30.513 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
that matches store photos to
written product descriptions.

00:00:30.513 --> 00:00:34.951 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
For this soup, the description it chose
named the right brand and the wrong flavor.

00:00:35.731 --> 00:00:40.531 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We present A Transferable Variant Signal
That Grocery Retrieval Cannot Reach.

00:00:41.311 --> 00:00:43.999 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
One wrong flavor leaves
us with three questions.

00:00:44.619 --> 00:00:48.613 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
First, could the model distinguish
the flavors in a photo even

00:00:48.613 --> 00:00:50.251 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
when it chose the wrong description?

00:00:50.871 --> 00:00:52.736 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Second, can a distinction learned

00:00:52.736 --> 00:00:56.161 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
from one brand help identify a
product from a different brand?

00:00:56.781 --> 00:01:01.432 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Third, how much does that help when the
only choices are flavors of the same brand?

00:01:02.812 --> 00:01:07.871 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Look at this can. It is Campbell's Creamy
Tomato. The other can is Chicken Noodle.

00:01:08.271 --> 00:01:11.771 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
A vision encoder turns a
photo into a list of numbers.

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:14.417 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
These measurements are called image features.

00:01:14.417 --> 00:01:17.171 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
SigLIP2 also has a text encoder.

00:01:17.171 --> 00:01:21.055 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
It turns each written description
into a list of text features.

00:01:21.055 --> 00:01:25.674 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The model ranks the descriptions by
how closely each text vector aligns

00:01:25.674 --> 00:01:26.772 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
with the photo vector.

00:01:27.172 --> 00:01:30.565 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
For the tomato soup photo,
Chicken Noodle ranked first.

00:01:30.565 --> 00:01:33.317 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The model got the brand
right and the flavor wrong.

00:01:33.717 --> 00:01:35.969 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
In an earlier GroceryVision study,

00:01:35.969 --> 00:01:38.419 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
SigLIP2 ranked the correct item among the

00:01:38.419 --> 00:01:43.020 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
first five descriptions
for 94.5 percent of photos.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:46.305 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
It ranked the correct
item first for 77 percent.

00:01:47.085 --> 00:01:51.427 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The photo passes through a vision
encoder. We freeze its weights.

00:01:51.427 --> 00:01:52.574 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The snowflake means

00:01:52.574 --> 00:01:56.653 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
that the same photo will give the same
measurements throughout the experiment.

00:01:57.053 --> 00:02:00.155 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We train a simple classifier
to predict the flavor

00:02:00.155 --> 00:02:03.677 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
from those image features.
This is a linear probe.

00:02:03.677 --> 00:02:07.149 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Only its weights change.
It never reads the catalog.

00:02:07.549 --> 00:02:10.936 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We test the probe on other
photos of the same brands.

00:02:10.936 --> 00:02:14.728 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Its score gives each variety
equal weight within its brand.

00:02:14.728 --> 00:02:18.676 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The probe reaches 97.1
percent balanced accuracy

00:02:18.676 --> 00:02:21.562 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
with correct training
labels. Shuffling the labels

00:02:21.562 --> 00:02:26.925 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
within each training brand lowers its score
to about 35 percent, close to guessing.

00:02:27.325 --> 00:02:31.885 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
This result shows that the encoder
retained information about the variety.

00:02:31.885 --> 00:02:35.597 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Matching a photo to a description
can still give the wrong flavor.

00:02:36.377 --> 00:02:39.615 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Could a distinction learned from
one brand help with another?

00:02:39.615 --> 00:02:42.644 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We began with brands for
which we had labeled photos.

00:02:43.264 --> 00:02:46.992 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
First, we average the photo
measurements for each variety.

00:02:46.992 --> 00:02:50.827 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Then we average those variety
measurements within each brand.

00:02:50.827 --> 00:02:54.424 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The arrow from the brand
average to one variety shows how

00:02:54.424 --> 00:02:56.928 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
that variety differs from
the rest of its brand.

00:02:57.548 --> 00:03:00.849 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Now imagine laying a ruler along those arrows.

00:03:00.849 --> 00:03:04.151 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Along some directions the
varieties spread apart.

00:03:04.151 --> 00:03:08.177 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We kept 64 directions that
captured the most variation.

00:03:08.177 --> 00:03:10.311 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Together they form the variant subspace.

00:03:10.931 --> 00:03:14.808 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The calculation is called
singular value decomposition.

00:03:14.808 --> 00:03:18.877 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The columns of V give directions
in the image feature space.

00:03:18.877 --> 00:03:23.565 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Sigma measures how much the training
varieties vary along each direction.

00:03:23.565 --> 00:03:28.003 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
U gives the relative weight of each
training variety along each direction.

00:03:28.783 --> 00:03:32.240 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Does it matter which photos
we group as the same variety?

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:36.379 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
To find out, we repeat the calculation
using the same training photos.

00:03:36.779 --> 00:03:40.172 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We shuffled the labels among
photos of the same brand.

00:03:40.172 --> 00:03:43.779 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
A tomato photo could now
carry a chicken noodle label.

00:03:43.779 --> 00:03:47.448 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The photos stayed in place, and
each name appeared just as often.

00:03:47.848 --> 00:03:52.479 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We learn a second set of 64 directions
from the shuffled training labels.

00:03:52.479 --> 00:03:57.236 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We test both sets on the same photos,
whose correct labels we keep for scoring.

00:03:58.016 --> 00:04:02.458 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Now we try the directions on three
coffee roasts. No Starbucks photo

00:04:02.458 --> 00:04:05.399 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
or description was used
to learn these directions.

00:04:05.799 --> 00:04:09.646 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We project the SigLIP2 image
vector for each coffee photo

00:04:09.646 --> 00:04:14.269 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
and the text vector for each
description onto the same 64 directions.

00:04:14.269 --> 00:04:17.364 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
It is like measuring both
with the same set of rulers.

00:04:17.364 --> 00:04:22.143 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Learning those directions on other brands
and applying them here is subspace transfer.

00:04:22.543 --> 00:04:25.083 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
These are two real photos of Sumatra.

00:04:25.083 --> 00:04:28.433 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
With the original labels,
the first matches Sumatra.

00:04:28.433 --> 00:04:32.851 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The second matches Breakfast Blend.
The roast is the same in both photos.

00:04:33.251 --> 00:04:35.983 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Each of these ten photos shows Sumatra.

00:04:35.983 --> 00:04:39.675 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
With the original training labels,
nine out of ten were correct.

00:04:39.675 --> 00:04:43.196 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
With shuffled training labels,
zero out of ten were correct.

00:04:43.196 --> 00:04:45.799 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The model predicted Pike
Place for every photo.

00:04:46.199 --> 00:04:50.021 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We chose this coffee example
because correct training labels

00:04:50.021 --> 00:04:53.880 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
made the largest difference
among the 28 cases we examined.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:56.653 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
To see whether the result holds more broadly,

00:04:56.653 --> 00:04:59.533 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
we look at all the test brands
with at least two varieties.

00:05:00.313 --> 00:05:02.768 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Each row is a vision-language model.

00:05:02.768 --> 00:05:06.844 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We fit directions on one group of
brands and test on other brands.

00:05:06.844 --> 00:05:10.292 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We calculate first-match
accuracy for each test brand

00:05:10.292 --> 00:05:13.969 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
and average equally across
brands, then across ten trials.

00:05:14.589 --> 00:05:19.625 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
SigLIP2 compares its image and
text features along 64 directions.

00:05:19.625 --> 00:05:25.023 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Average first-match accuracy is
51.7 percent with directions learned

00:05:25.023 --> 00:05:28.923 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
from correct training labels and 38.1 percent

00:05:28.923 --> 00:05:31.509 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
with directions learned from shuffled labels.

00:05:31.509 --> 00:05:35.435 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The last column uses all the
original image and text features.

00:05:36.055 --> 00:05:40.920 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Now take the same test photos and remove
every other brand from the catalog.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:45.485 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
For a coffee photo, the choices are now
only the roasts of that coffee brand.

00:05:46.105 --> 00:05:51.424 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Correct training labels improve
SigLIP2’s average first-match accuracy by

00:05:51.424 --> 00:05:56.880 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
13.6 percentage points over shuffled
labels across the full catalog.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.649 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
For the same photos, the gain is 3.9 points

00:06:00.649 --> 00:06:03.452 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
when we rank only the
correct brand’s descriptions.

00:06:03.452 --> 00:06:09.448 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
MobileCLIP gains 4.5 points across the
full catalog and 1.2 within the brand.

00:06:09.448 --> 00:06:13.373 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
For these two models, most of the
gain came from distinguishing brands.

00:06:14.153 --> 00:06:16.137 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Let us return to the three questions.

00:06:16.757 --> 00:06:22.283 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Did the encoder record the flavor? Yes. A
simple classifier could read the variety

00:06:22.283 --> 00:06:25.336 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
from a photo even when
matching it to text failed.

00:06:25.956 --> 00:06:30.954 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Did the directions transfer? Yes.
On brands excluded from the fit,

00:06:30.954 --> 00:06:34.554 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
all nine models ranked the correct
description first more often,

00:06:34.554 --> 00:06:38.396 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
on average, with correct training
labels than with shuffled labels.

00:06:39.016 --> 00:06:43.914 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
What if every choice came from the
correct brand? For SigLIP2 and MobileCLIP,

00:06:43.914 --> 00:06:47.339 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
about 30 percent of the gain
over shuffled labels remained.

00:06:47.339 --> 00:06:51.136 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
That is the part that helped
distinguish varieties of the same brand.

00:06:52.516 --> 00:06:57.021 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
A receipt needs the exact variety.
To evaluate a checkout model,

00:06:57.021 --> 00:06:59.737 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
give it several flavors of the same brand.

00:06:59.737 --> 00:07:03.044 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
That test reveals errors a
whole-catalog score can hide.

00:07:03.664 --> 00:07:08.139 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
For representation learning, a wrong
answer leaves two possibilities.

00:07:08.139 --> 00:07:10.286 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The encoder may have missed the difference,

00:07:10.286 --> 00:07:13.019 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
or the comparison with
text may have missed it.

00:07:13.019 --> 00:07:17.624 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Testing the image on its own lets
us distinguish them. In this study,

00:07:17.624 --> 00:07:21.589 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
the encoder retained a distinction
that catalog matching often missed.

00:07:22.209 --> 00:07:26.812 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
These are the same ten Sumatra photos
and the same written descriptions.

00:07:26.812 --> 00:07:31.131 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Switching from directions learned with
shuffled labels to directions learned

00:07:31.131 --> 00:07:35.706 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
with correct labels changes the closest
roast for the average photo vector

00:07:35.706 --> 00:07:37.948 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
from Pike Place to Sumatra.

00:07:38.568 --> 00:07:39.869 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Thank you for listening.
